"""Hand-written English method descriptions, part 1: equipment category Other.

Keyed by the v5 catalog slug (slugify of the canonical exercise name).
Merged by build_progression.py as the last description tier; a key that
matches no catalog row fails the build, so typos surface immediately.
"""

DESCRIPTIONS = {
    "air_bike": "The air bike is a full-body conditioning machine that drives a fan with both the arms and the legs, so resistance scales with effort rather than with a dial. Short, hard intervals build work capacity while easy spinning serves as low-impact recovery. Keep the chest tall and push and pull the handles in rhythm with the pedals.",
    "back_lever": "The back lever is an advanced gymnastics hold performed hanging from rings or a bar with the body turned face-down and extended horizontally. It demands strong lats, biceps and straight-arm strength along with shoulder mobility, so it is progressed through tucked and single-leg variations before the full position.",
    "battle_rope_double_slam": "The battle rope double slam drives both ropes overhead and slams them to the floor in one explosive motion, training the shoulders, core and hips as a single chain. It is usually performed for time or high reps, making it a staple of conditioning circuits and metabolic finishers.",
    "bench_adductor_stretch": "The bench adductor stretch opens the inner thighs by placing one leg on a bench and folding the torso toward it. Keep the standing knee soft and hinge from the hips rather than rounding the back, easing into the stretch until the adductors release.",
    "bench_ankle_stretch": "The bench ankle stretch improves dorsiflexion by placing the ball of one foot on a bench and driving the knee forward over the toes. Keep the heel down and the movement slow, using short pulsing leans to loosen a stiff ankle before squatting or lunging.",
    "bench_bulgarian_split_stretch": "The bench Bulgarian split stretch lengthens the hip flexors of the rear leg by draping that foot on a bench behind you and sinking the hips down and forward. Squeeze the rear glute to deepen the stretch safely; it doubles as the setup position for Bulgarian split squats.",
    "bench_calf_stretch": "The bench calf stretch places the forefoot on a bench with the heel anchored to the floor, then leans the torso in to lengthen the calf. Straighten the knee to bias the gastrocnemius or bend it slightly to reach the soleus.",
    "bench_chest_stretch": "The bench chest stretch lies face-up across a bench with the arms opened wide, letting gravity pull the shoulders toward the floor. It is a gentle way to open a tight chest after pressing work; bend the elbows more to shift the emphasis toward the shoulders.",
    "bench_child_s_pose": "The bench child's pose kneels in front of a bench and folds the arms onto it, lengthening the lats and spine without loading the knees. Breathe slowly into the sides of the ribcage and let the hips sink back with each exhale.",
    "bench_couch_stretch": "The bench couch stretch is a quad and hip-flexor opener that sets the rear foot up on a bench behind the body, then raises the torso upright. Tuck the pelvis and squeeze the rear glute before leaning back; it is one of the most effective stretches for desk-tightened hips.",
    "bench_figure_4_glute_stretch": "The bench figure-4 glute stretch crosses one ankle over the opposite knee in a seated position and folds forward, stretching the glutes and hip rotators. Keep the back long rather than rounded and pull the crossed knee gently toward the chest to intensify it.",
    "bench_hamstring_stretch": "The bench hamstring stretch rests one straight leg on a bench and hinges the chest toward the thigh. Keep both hips square and the knee of the elevated leg unlocked to target the hamstring without straining its attachments.",
    "bench_lat_stretch": "The bench lat stretch grips a bench with both hands and sinks the hips back and down, letting the torso fall between the arms. Push the chest toward the floor and rotate the palms slightly outward to widen the stretch through the lats.",
    "boat_pose": "Boat pose is a yoga hold that balances on the sitting bones with the legs and torso forming a V. It builds isometric strength through the deep abdominals and hip flexors; keep the chest lifted and the spine long rather than rounding to bring the legs higher.",
    "bow_pose": "Bow pose lies face-down, bends the knees and reaches back to grip the ankles, lifting the chest and thighs into an arch. It stretches the front of the body while strengthening the spinal erectors; keep the knees hip-width and avoid forcing the lift.",
    "butterfly_stretch": "The butterfly stretch sits with the soles of the feet together and lets the knees fall toward the floor, opening the inner thighs and groin. Keep the spine long and hinge slightly from the hips rather than rounding; pressing gently on the knees deepens the stretch, and never bounce.",
    "camel_pose": "Camel pose kneels upright and reaches back to the heels, opening the chest and shoulders into a deep backbend. Press the hips forward over the knees and keep the glutes engaged so the bend comes from the upper back rather than hinging at the lumbar spine.",
    "cat_stretch": "The cat stretch rounds and arches the spine on all fours in a slow, controlled rhythm. It mobilizes the whole vertebral column and re-establishes awareness of the core; sync each movement with a full breath cycle.",
    "chair_pose": "Chair pose holds a deep quarter squat with the arms overhead, loading the quads, glutes and shoulders isometrically. Sit the hips back, keep the weight in the heels and draw the lower ribs in so the back stays long.",
    "chin_tuck_hold": "The chin tuck hold lies on the back and gently draws the chin toward the throat, holding the position to strengthen the deep neck flexors. It is a common corrective for forward-head posture and is usually held for 15 to 30 seconds at a time.",
    "clamshell_hold": "The clamshell hold keeps the top knee of a side-lying clamshell open and suspended, turning the exercise into an isometric for the hip abductors. The leg should stay light; if the pelvis rocks backward, the glute is no longer doing the work.",
    "cobra_stretch": "The cobra stretch lies face-down and presses the chest up with straight arms or a gentle lift, opening the abdominals and hip flexors. Keep the shoulders away from the ears and let the neck stay neutral; small doses after sitting or heavy squats feel best.",
    "cow_face_pose": "Cow face pose stacks the knees and binds the hands behind the back, opening the outer hips and shoulders at the same time. Use a strap if the hands cannot meet, and switch sides to keep both hips even.",
    "crescent_lunge": "The crescent lunge holds a deep forward lunge with the back knee lifted and the arms reaching overhead. It opens the hip flexors of the rear leg while the front leg and core hold the balance; keep the front knee stacked over the ankle.",
    "crow_pose": "Crow pose balances the shins on the backs of the upper arms with the feet off the floor, training shoulder stability, wrist strength and core control. Start with one foot at a time or a block under the toes; look slightly forward, not straight down.",
    "dancer_pose": "Dancer pose balances on one leg while holding the opposite foot behind the body, kicking the lifted leg into the hand to open the quad and shoulder. Fix the gaze on a point and hinge the torso forward gradually; it challenges balance as much as flexibility.",
    "dead_bug_hold": "The dead bug hold keeps the opposite arm and leg hovering off the floor in a static version of the dead bug. Press the lower back into the ground throughout; the moment it arches, the range is too long for the core to control.",
    "dead_hang": "The dead hang simply hangs from a pull-up bar with straight arms, building grip strength, shoulder health and the lat mobility needed for pulling work. Let the shoulders rise toward the ears at the start and settle into a relaxed, active hang.",
    "dolphin_pose": "Dolphin pose sets up like downward dog but rests on the forearms, shifting the load into the shoulders and upper back. It is the standard strength drill behind headstand and forearm-stand progressions; press the floor away and keep the ribs knitted in.",
    "doorway_chest_stretch": "The doorway chest stretch places the forearms on a door frame and steps through until the chest opens. Elbow height decides the emphasis, lower for the lower chest and higher for the shoulders, so it is a quick, equipment-light fix for rounded posture.",
    "downward_facing_dog": "Downward-facing dog forms an inverted V with the body, stretching the calves and hamstrings while the shoulders and arms bear weight. Press the floor away, reach the hips back and let the heels drift toward the ground rather than forcing them down.",
    "downward_dog_pedal": "The downward dog pedal bends one knee while straightening the other in a slow alternating rhythm, warming the calves and hamstrings inside the downward dog position. It is a staple early stretch before lower-body training.",
    "downward_dog_to_low_lunge": "This flow steps one foot from downward dog into a low lunge and back, opening the hip flexors of the trailing leg and the hamstrings of the leading one. Move with the breath and keep the transitions smooth; it is an ideal warm-up sequence before squats or runs.",
    "eagle_pose": "Eagle pose wraps one leg over the other and binds the arms in front of the chest, holding the tangled balance. It stretches the outer hips and between the shoulder blades while demanding steady single-leg control; swap the wrap direction to even both sides.",
    "easy_pose": "Easy pose is a simple seated cross-legged position used to settle posture and breathing. Sit tall on the sitting bones, let the knees drop toward the floor and alternate the leg crossing to keep the hips symmetrical.",
    "extended_side_angle_pose": "Extended side angle pose sinks into a wide lunge and lays the same-side forearm on the thigh, reaching the top arm over the ear to lengthen the whole side body. Keep the chest open toward the ceiling rather than collapsing forward.",
    "fish_pose": "Fish pose lies on the back and arches the chest toward the ceiling with the crown of the head resting lightly on the floor. It opens the front of the neck, chest and shoulders after long sitting; keep the weight on the elbows, never on the head.",
    "front_lever": "The front lever hangs from a bar or rings and raises the straight body to horizontal, face-up. It is one of the most demanding straight-arm skills, loading the lats and core enormously; tuck, then advance through one-leg variations before the full hold.",
    "garland_pose": "Garland pose settles into a deep squat with the heels down and the elbows pressing the knees outward. It opens the adductors, ankles and hips and is an excellent diagnostic of squat mobility; elevate the heels slightly if they lift.",
    "glute_bridge_hold": "The glute bridge hold lifts the hips into full extension and keeps them there, squeezing the glutes continuously. Push through the heels, tuck the pelvis and stop short of arching the lower back; holds of 20 to 45 seconds build endurance in the posterior chain.",
    "glute_kickback_hold": "The glute kickback hold suspends a leg extended behind the body from a quadruped position, holding it against gravity. Keep the hips square to the floor; height matters less than a locked-in pelvis. It is a glute isolation drill rather than a leg lift.",
    "half_kneeling_hip_flexor_rock": "The half-kneeling hip flexor rock rocks the hips forward and back from a half-kneeling stance, rhythmically loading and releasing the front of the rear hip. Keep the glute of the rear leg squeezed and the ribs down; it is a controlled mobility drill, not a stretch-and-forget hold.",
    "half_moon_pose": "Half moon pose balances on one leg with the torso tipped to horizontal and the free leg lifted to hip height or above. It trains balance and hip stability while opening the standing-side hamstring; a block under the lower hand keeps the spine long.",
    "happy_baby_pose": "Happy baby pose lies on the back, grips the outsides of the feet and draws the knees toward the armpits. It releases the adductors and lower back gently; rock side to side for a light spinal massage and keep the tailbone heavy on the floor.",
    "head_to_knee_pose": "Head-to-knee pose folds one straight leg toward the chest while hinging over the extended other leg. Keep the lengthened spine rather than chasing the shin with a rounded back; it stretches the hamstring and calf of the extended leg.",
    "hero_pose": "Hero pose kneels between the feet with the calves alongside the thighs, stretching the quads and ankles deeply. Sit on a block if the hips cannot reach the floor and keep the spine upright; short holds are enough at first.",
    "high_plank": "The high plank holds the top of a push-up with straight arms, stacking the shoulders over the wrists and bracing the whole trunk. Push the floor away, squeeze the glutes and keep the neck long; it is both a core drill and the setup for push-up variations.",
    "human_flag": "The human flag grips a vertical pole and holds the straight body sideways off it. It demands exceptional oblique and shoulder strength; the progression runs through tucked, straddle and negative versions long before the full flag.",
    "incline_treadmill_walk": "The incline treadmill walk sets the belt to a steep grade and walks at a steady pace, turning a low-impact stroll into a demanding conditioning tool. The glutes and calves do much of the work; resist the urge to grip the handrails, which unloads the legs.",
    "isometric_neck_lateral_flexion": "Isometric neck lateral flexion presses the hand against the side of the head while the neck resists sideways bending. Train both sides evenly and keep the pressure modest; steady holds build the neck strength that contact sports and heavy pulls demand.",
    "jump_rope": "Jump rope turns a rope over the head and under the feet in continuous small hops, training the calves, shoulders and coordination while spiking the heart rate. Stay on the balls of the feet with low, quiet bounces; it scales from easy warm-up rounds to brutal intervals.",
    "knee_to_chest_stretch": "The knee-to-chest stretch lies on the back and draws one or both knees in toward the chest. It decompresses the lower back and releases the glutes; keep the head relaxed on the floor and breathe into the stretch.",
    "kneeling_wrist_stretch": "The kneeling wrist stretch kneels and places the backs of the hands on the floor with fingers toward the knees, leaning gently to extend the wrists. It is a favorite of lifters and climbers; ease off if any position produces sharp pain.",
    "l_sit": "The L sit holds the straight legs out in front of the body while supported on the hands, on the floor or on parallel bars. It builds tremendous hip-flexor and triceps strength and core control; start with one leg tucked or brief 5-second holds.",
    "legs_up_the_wall_pose": "Legs-up-the-wall pose rests the legs vertically against a wall while the back lies on the floor. It drains fatigue from the legs, relaxes the hamstrings and lower back, and is a classic recovery posture after hard training days.",
    "lizard_stretch": "The lizard stretch drops into a deep low lunge with both hands inside the front foot, sinking the hips toward the floor. It opens the hip flexors and adductors at once; forearms down or the back knee lifted both work, whichever keeps the front knee tracking out.",
    "locust_pose": "Locust pose lies face-down and lifts the chest, arms and legs against gravity, strengthening the entire back side of the body. Keep the lift modest and the neck neutral; it is one of the safest ways to build spinal-erector endurance.",
    "low_lunge": "The low lunge rests the back knee on the floor in a long split stance, opening the hip flexors of the trailing leg. Keep the torso tall and tuck the pelvis before deepening; adding a gentle side bend or reach multiplies the stretch.",
    "low_lunge_to_half_split": "This flow rocks from a low lunge into a half split and back, alternating a hip-flexor stretch with a hamstring stretch in one continuous movement. Move slowly with the breath; it is a highly efficient warm-up for running, squatting and kicking patterns.",
    "mountain_pose": "Mountain pose is standing tall with the weight balanced evenly across both feet and the crown of the head reaching up. It looks like nothing, but it trains the posture, breath and alignment that every other exercise inherits; scan the body from the ground up.",
    "neck_side_stretch": "The neck side stretch tilts the ear toward the shoulder, optionally assisting with a light hand. Keep the opposite shoulder heavy and relaxed; 20 to 30 seconds per side releases the upper trapezius after desk time or heavy carries.",
    "nordic_hamstring_curl": "The Nordic hamstring curl anchors the ankles and lowers the body forward from the knees with straight hips, fighting the fall with the hamstrings alone. It is one of the most effective hamstring-strength and injury-prevention drills in sport; control the descent and push back up only as far as strength allows.",
    "overhead_triceps_stretch": "The overhead triceps stretch reaches one arm overhead and bends the elbow so the hand drops between the shoulder blades, then assists with the opposite hand. Keep the ribs down, since arching the back fakes range; it is the go-to reset after pressing or locking out overhead.",
    "pilates_roll_down": "The Pilates roll down peels the spine off the floor one vertebra at a time and returns the same way, articulating the entire column. Move slowly enough that each segment gets its moment; it teaches segmental control that carries into every hinge and squat.",
    "pilates_saw": "The Pilates saw sits with the legs wide and rotates the torso over one leg, reaching the opposite hand past the little toe. Keep the sitting bones planted and rotate from the waist rather than collapsing; it combines spinal rotation with a hamstring stretch.",
    "pilates_spine_stretch_forward": "The Pilates spine stretch forward sits tall with the legs extended and curls the spine forward over the thighs one segment at a time. Reach long rather than round hard; it lengthens the back line of the body while reinforcing deep-core engagement.",
    "pilates_spine_twist": "The Pilates spine twist rotates the upright torso from side to side on a tall, still lower body. The pelvis stays perfectly level and only the ribs and head turn; it sharpens rotational control that transfers to throws, swings and punches.",
    "planche": "The planche supports the entire body horizontally on the hands with straight arms and no contact but the palms. It is a years-long skill progression loading the shoulders, wrists and core beyond almost any other bodyweight hold; lean progressively and train tucked versions first.",
    "plate_pinch": "The plate pinch holds one or more weight plates between a straight thumb and fingers at the side. It is a pure grip-strength tool that hits the thumb side of the hand that most other grip work misses; 20 to 45 second holds with both hands work well.",
    "plate_pullover": "The plate pullover lies on a bench and arcs a weight plate from over the chest to behind the head and back, keeping the arms nearly straight. It stretches the lats and chest under load while training the core to keep the ribs down.",
    "plow_pose": "Plow pose rolls the legs overhead until the toes touch the floor behind the head, stretching the entire back line of the body. Support the back with the hands and keep the weight off the neck; roll out slowly and avoid it with any cervical discomfort.",
    "plyo_lunge": "The plyo lunge jumps to switch the legs mid-air and lands back in a lunge, repeating explosively. Land soft with the hips back and the front knee tracking over the shoe; it builds lower-body power and raises the heart rate fast.",
    "puppy_pose": "Puppy pose kneels with the hips over the knees and walks the hands forward, melting the chest toward the floor. It opens the lats, chest and thoracic spine while sparing the lower back; rest the forehead on the ground and breathe into the upper back.",
    "pyramid_pose": "Pyramid pose stands in a short forward split with both legs straight and folds the torso over the front leg. It delivers a clean hamstring and calf stretch; square the hips to the front foot and hinge at the hip crease, not the waist.",
    "reverse_plank": "The reverse plank faces the ceiling, supported on the hands and heels with the body straight. It strengthens the posterior chain through the glutes, hamstrings and spinal erectors while opening the chest and shoulders; push the hips high and keep the neck relaxed.",
    "reverse_tabletop_hold": "The reverse tabletop hold bridges on hands and feet with the shins vertical and the torso flat, holding the hips at tabletop height. It trains glute and shoulder endurance and opens the front of the shoulders; keep the wrists under the shoulders and squeeze the glutes to hold.",
    "revolved_chair_pose": "Revolved chair pose twists a chair squat, pressing the hands together and hooking one elbow outside the opposite knee. The legs hold the squat isometrically while the spine rotates; lengthen on the inhale and deepen the twist on the exhale.",
    "revolved_crescent_lunge": "Revolved crescent lunge adds a spinal twist to the high lunge, bringing opposite hands together beside the front foot or in prayer at the chest. It is a balance and rotation drill that opens the thoracic spine while the legs hold the stance.",
    "ring_dead_hang": "The ring dead hang hangs from gymnastic rings with straight arms, adding instability that ordinary bar hangs lack. The grip and shoulder stabilizers work overtime to control the rings; keep the body quiet and the rings from drifting.",
    "rowing_machine": "The rowing machine drives legs, then body, then arms to move the handle, and reverses the order on the recovery. Done well it is a full-body conditioning tool that spares the joints; drive with the hips, keep the lower back long and resist racing the flywheel.",
    "running": "Running is steady-state locomotion at whatever pace the legs can hold, building aerobic capacity, calf and quad endurance and bone density. Land under the hips with a quick, light cadence and increase volume gradually; it pairs with strength work rather than replacing it.",
    "corpse_pose": "Corpse pose lies flat on the back with the limbs heavy and the breath slow. It closes a session by dropping the heart rate and releasing residual tension; the discipline is doing nothing deliberately, scanning and softening the body piece by piece.",
    "seated_forward_fold": "The seated forward fold extends the legs and hinges the chest toward the toes, stretching the hamstrings and lower back. Lead with the sternum and keep the spine long; holding the feet or shins with a flat back beats yanking the head down.",
    "seated_spinal_twist": "The seated spinal twist plants one foot outside the opposite knee and rotates the upright torso toward it. Lengthen the spine on the inhale and rotate a little more on each exhale; it mobilizes the thoracic spine and the hips together.",
    "seated_straddle_stretch": "The seated straddle stretch opens the legs into a wide V and folds the chest toward the floor. Hinge from the hips with a long spine and walk the hands forward slowly; the adductors respond to patience far better than to force.",
    "side_lying_hip_abduction_hold": "The side-lying hip abduction hold keeps the top leg lifted from a side-lying position, isolating the hip abductors isometrically. Keep the leg in line with the body rather than drifting forward, and the pelvis still; the burn should sit deep in the side of the hip.",
    "side_lying_hip_adduction_hold": "The side-lying hip adduction hold lifts the bottom leg against the floor from a side-lying position and holds it up, targeting the inner thigh. Keep the hips stacked; the bottom adductors work hard for such a small range.",
    "side_plank_leg_lift_hold": "The side plank leg lift hold raises the top leg off the bottom one inside a side plank and keeps it there. It stacks a glute-medius challenge on top of the obliques' isometric work; if the hips sag, return to a plain side plank first.",
    "sled_row": "The sled row anchors a rope or straps to a loaded sled and rows the body toward it, or drags the sled hand over hand. It is a joint-friendly horizontal pull that loads the back without shearing the spine; keep the hips low and drive the elbows past the ribs.",
    "sphinx_pose": "Sphinx pose lies on the forearms with the elbows under the shoulders and the chest lifted into a gentle backbend. It restores the lumbar curve after sitting and prepares the spine for deeper extension; press the forearms down and keep the glutes soft.",
    "standing_calf_stretch": "The standing calf stretch braces the hands on a wall and steps one foot back, pressing that heel into the floor. A straight back knee biases the gastrocnemius while a bent knee reaches the soleus; hold each version for 20 to 30 seconds.",
    "standing_forward_fold": "The standing forward fold hinges at the hips and lets the torso hang toward the floor, releasing the hamstrings and back. Bend the knees as much as needed to keep the fold comfortable; gravity does the stretching, so no pulling is required.",
    "standing_forward_fold_to_half_lift": "This flow moves between a standing forward fold and a half lift with a flat back, teaching the hinge and lengthening the hamstrings in both directions. Flow with the breath; the flat-backed half position is where the hinge pattern is trained.",
    "standing_side_bend_flow": "The standing side bend flow reaches one arm overhead and leans side to side in a continuous rhythm, opening the obliques and the space between the ribs and hip. Keep both feet grounded and the hips still so the bend happens along the spine.",
    "standing_split": "The standing split folds forward and lifts one leg toward the ceiling as high as the hamstring allows. It is an intense hamstring stretch of the standing leg and a hip-flexor stretch of the lifted one; keep the hips level rather than chasing leg height.",
    "supine_spinal_twist": "The supine spinal twist lies on the back and drops the knees to one side while the shoulders stay heavy on the floor. It unwinds the lower back and chest after training; rest the top knee on a block if the floor is too far away.",
    "supported_shoulderstand": "The supported shoulderstand lifts the legs and hips overhead with the hands supporting the back, resting the weight on the shoulders. It is a gentle inversion that stretches the neck and upper back; enter and exit slowly and keep the head still.",
    "thoracic_bridge": "The thoracic bridge pushes the hips up while reaching one arm overhead to rotate the upper back through the bridge. It restores rotation to a stiff mid-back and stretches the chest; keep the supporting shoulder stacked over the wrist.",
    "thread_the_needle": "Thread the needle slides one arm under the chest from all fours, resting the shoulder and ear on the floor. It rotates the thoracic spine and stretches between the shoulder blades; press the top hand down to deepen gently.",
    "thread_the_needle_flow": "The thread the needle flow alternates reaching one arm to the ceiling and threading it under the body, opening and closing thoracic rotation in rhythm. Move with the breath; it is a reliable warm-up before any pressing, throwing or rotational work.",
    "three_legged_downward_dog": "The three-legged downward dog lifts one leg toward the ceiling from a downward dog, loading the standing hip and opening the lifted one. Keep the hips as square as the hamstring allows and press firmly through both hands.",
    "tree_pose": "Tree pose balances on one leg with the other foot pressed to the inner thigh and the hands in prayer overhead. Press the foot and thigh into each other and fix the gaze on a still point; wobbling is the drill working.",
    "triangle_pose": "Triangle pose stands in a wide stance and tips the torso over the front leg, stacking the hands vertically toward the floor and ceiling. Lengthen both sides of the waist equally; the stretch belongs to the hamstrings and the side body together.",
    "trx_plank": "The TRX plank sets the feet in the straps and holds a plank with the body in one line. The unstable suspension forces the core to fight rotation and sagging simultaneously; keep the shoulders away from the ears and the straps quiet.",
    "trx_side_plank": "The TRX side plank holds a side plank with the feet suspended in the straps. The obliques and hip stabilizers work against the straps' drift; keep the body long and the bottom shoulder packed.",
    "upward_facing_dog": "Upward-facing dog presses the chest up from the floor with straight arms and the thighs hovering, opening the front of the body. Keep the shoulders down away from the ears and push the tops of the feet into the floor to spare the lower back.",
    "walking": "Walking is the lowest-cost conditioning there is: a brisk, steady pace that builds the aerobic base and aids recovery between harder sessions. Keep the pace honest enough to raise the breath; hills or a weighted vest add load without impact.",
    "warrior_i": "Warrior I squares the hips forward in a long lunge with the arms reaching overhead. It opens the hip flexors of the back leg while the front leg, core and shoulders hold the frame; keep the back heel heavy or lifted consistently on one side.",
    "warrior_iii": "Warrior III balances on one leg with the torso and the other leg forming a horizontal T. The standing glute and hamstring carry everything while the core keeps the spine long; reach through the back heel as actively as the fingertips.",
    "warrior_ii": "Warrior II holds a wide bent-knee stance with the arms extended in a T and the gaze over the front hand. It builds endurance through the quads and hips while opening the chest; sink the front thigh toward parallel and keep the torso upright.",
    "weighted_dips": "Weighted dips add a plate or dumbbell to the standard chest dip, pushing the triceps and lower chest into serious overload. Keep the lean forward, the elbows tracking back and the range honest; add load only once bodyweight reps are comfortable.",
    "weighted_wall_crunch": "The weighted wall crunch lies with the legs up a wall and crunches a plate or dumbbell from the chest toward the feet. The wall fixes the hips so the abs do the whole job; curl the ribs toward the pelvis rather than pulling the head.",
    "wide_legged_forward_fold": "The wide-legged forward fold stands with the feet wide and folds the torso down between the legs, stretching the adductors and hamstrings together. Keep the weight in the mid-foot and the spine long; hands to the floor or to the ankles both work.",
    "wrist_roller": "The wrist roller winds a weighted rope up by rolling a handle over and over, burning the forearms through their full range. Roll in both directions and resist on the way down; it is the classic finisher for grip day.",
}
